.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd October 8, 2026
.Dt RG 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm rg
.Nd search files for a pattern (ripgrep)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar pattern
.Op Ar path ...
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar pattern
.Fl
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is ripgrep 15.2.0, built unmodified from its published source.
It searches the named files, or the named directories recursively, for
lines matching a regular expression and prints them.
It is the search tool for directory trees;
.Xr grep 1
(sbase) searches files and standard input with POSIX regular expressions
and reads a pipe without being told.
.Pp
What has been qualified on ABSD (the matrix in
.Pa scripts/cmds-matrix.py ) :
.Bl -bullet
.It
one file, several files in the order given, and a directory tree
.Pq with Fl -sort Ar path
with the usual flags
.Fl n , Fl c , Fl l , Fl i , Fl o , Fl a ,
and
.Fl e ;
.It
standard input, when the path is written as
.Ql - ;
.It
the exit status: 0 when a line matched, 1 when none did, 2 for an error
(a missing path, a bad flag, no pattern), which the shell reports as
.Ql absd-sh: rg: exit status Ar n ;
.It
a binary file (one holding a NUL byte) named on the command line is reported
as
.Ql binary file matches
and skipped silently in a tree walk;
.Fl a
searches it as text.
.El
.Pp
Everything else ripgrep can do is upstream behaviour that has merely not
been exercised here; it is not refused, but it is not qualified either.
.Sh DIFFERENCES FROM LINUX
.Bl -bullet
.It
Standard input is searched only when named as
.Ql - .
Without a path,
.Nm
cannot tell on ABSD that its standard input is a pipe (ripgrep's test for
that exists only for Unix and Windows) and searches the working directory
instead.
So write
.Ql cat f | rg pattern - ,
never
.Ql cat f | rg pattern .
.It
There are no threads.
.Nm
notices and uses one; with an explicit
.Fl j
greater than one on a directory it stops with
.Ql rg: unsupported platform ,
status 2.
.It
Standard output is never a terminal, so there is no colour, no grouping of
matches under a file name, and no paging; the output is the
.Ql file:line
form, as under a pipe on Linux.
.It
The text after an I/O error is the ABSD message
.Pq Ql ABSD path not found ,
not
.Ql \&No such file or directory .
.It
Files are not memory-mapped (there is no such mechanism); every file is
read through the file server, so a large tree takes time.
.It
.Nm
is about 2.3 MB and, like every program, is read whole through the file
server before it starts: it starts more slowly than the native commands
(1 to 3 s under QEMU TCG, where a native command takes 0.1 s); see
.Xr intro 1 .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Lines of a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ rg -n "line 1[0-9]" /etc/lines.txt
10:line 10
11:line 11
.Ed
.Pp
A log under
.Pa /var/log
.Pq Xr afterboot 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ rg -c "session" /var/log/sshd
absd$ rg -n "RECOVERY|ESCALATED" /var/log
.Ed
.Pp
Which files under a directory mention something, in path order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ rg -l --sort path operator /home/operator
.Ed
.Pp
Standard input, in a pipeline:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
absd$ cat /etc/network.conf | rg -i address -
absd$ rg -o "row 000[0-9][0-9]" /etc/big.txt | wc -l
.Ed
.Pp
From the host, in one command, with the exit status as ripgrep's:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ssh -p 2222 operator@127.0.0.1 rg -c started /var/log/fsd
.Ed
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when at least one line matched, 1 when none did, 2 on an error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr absd-sh 1 ,
.Xr grep 1 ,
.Xr intro 1 ,
.Xr filesystem 7
.Pp
The upstream manual,
.Ql rg --help ,
describes every flag; only what this page lists has been exercised on ABSD.
